                        <forename>John Hume</forename>
                        <surname>Spry</surname>
                        <addName/>
                    </persName>
                    <birth when="1000-01-01">
                        <placeName/>
                    </birth>
                    <death when="1000-12-31"/>
                    <occupation>Schoolteacher/Educationalist</occupation>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p>Possibly Rev. John Hume Spry, whom the Dictionary of Living Authors
                            (1816) gives as living in Bath and then Birmingham (although the contacts suggest someone who comes to town only occasionally). Godwin's interest
                            in him might be in relation to William junr's schooling, since he takes
                            William with him, when William would have been about 8. There was an
                            infant school for the Parish of Mary-le-bone some years later (est 1828
                            by the Rev Dr Spry), and it is possible that Spry's earlier writings may
                            have interested Godwin.</p>
                    </note>
                    <bibl type="MS"> </bibl>
                    <bibl type="printedbook">Thomas Smith<title>A Topographical and Historical Account of the Parish of St Mary-le-bone </title>, London,
                        John Smith, 1833</bibl>
